[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;231m\u001b[2m 101 \u001b[22m   \u001b[38;5;148mit\u001b[38;5;231m(\u001b[38;5;186m'keeps the order of the file, one request per invoice'\u001b[38;5;231m, \u001b[38;5;148masync\u001b[38;5;231m () =>\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m     \u001b[22m  {\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;231m\u001b[2m 102 \u001b[22m     \u001b[38;5;81mconst\u001b[38;5;231m model = \u001b[38;5;148mfakeModel\u001b[38;5;231m(\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;246m●\u001b[3G\u001b[39mFinal typecheck, tests, and build\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;5;246m  ⎿  \u001b[39m\u001b[2m\u001b[3mAll good. Let me do a final check of the working tree and think about anything \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bmissed. Issue checklist: - Add \u001b[38;5;153minvoicer review <file>\u001b[39m: yes. - Asks model whether each\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bline of each invoice says clearly enough what we delivered: yes, one request per \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Binvoice, verdicts per line. - Prints the lines that don't, with why: yes. - Exits 1 \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bwhen it finds one: yes. - Run in CI on invoices/: yes. - Agree with Maria: 100% on \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B3-run eval (60/60 vague caught, 60/60 clear passed). - Don't block good invoices: \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B100% clear pass. Should we run \u001b[38;5;153mnpm run eval\u001b[39m (categorize) to ensure the refactor \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Bdidn't break categorize? We ran unit tests and typecheck; the model client/settings \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m \u001b[3G   \u001b[2m\u001b[3mmove doesn't change behavior. But we could run it quickly to be safe? It's 3 runs × \r\u001b[1B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m     \u001b[2m\u001b[3m20 cases = 60 calls. We already ran 60 clarity calls. Costs a few cents. It might be…\r\u001b[2B\u001b[23m\u001b[22m\u001b[38;5;174m·\u001b[45G\u001b[38;5;248mthinking\u001b[39m\u001b[34;1H\u001b[32;3H"]
